    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d407">

                <pb facs="0700" n="686" sameAs="frus:frus1949v05/pg686"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.002/12–2149: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kirk</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-12-21T10:00:00+03:00">December 21,
                            1949—10 a. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3156. Sending following letter to Vishinsky December 21 (Deptel 924, December 15<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d407-f1"/>): “I have the honor on behalf of the Government of the
                    United States of America to request you to transmit to Generalissimo Stalin,
                    Chairman of the Council of Ministers of Union of Soviet Socialist Republics,
                    best wishes on the occasion of his seventieth birthday”.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d407-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d407-f1" n="1" xml:id="d407-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed. The Department of State herein told Ambassador Kirk that no
                    congratulatory message had been sent to Stalin in 1939. However, in view of the
                    extensive, official preparations under way this time, the Ambassador was now
                    given discretion to deliver a greeting of best wishes for Stalin on instructions
                    of his government. (861.002/12–1049)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d407-f2" n="2" xml:id="d407-f2-content">
                <p>Ambassador Kirk informed the Department in airgram A–1268 from Moscow on December
                    29 that both <hi rendition="#italic">Pravda</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Izvestiya</hi> had printed this felicitation on the front page of their
                    issues for December 25. He then remarked: “In this connection it may be of
                    interest to note that the United States was, as far as can be ascertained from
                    the Soviet press, the only government whose congratulations were expressed in a
                    letter signed by its Ambassador, all others having been signed by Chiefs of
                    State, Prime Ministers or Foreign Ministers.” (861.002/12–2949)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
